        "description": "Played branch: low-to-moderate slot pressure. In January, mobile allocation pressure is present but not universally delay-inducing; a minority of mobile users encounter slots at or near their thresholds. Some commuter and habitual-app users redirect spend to Dutch Bros or Dunkin on individual mornings, but the two-event switching thresholds are crossed by a fraction of affected users rather than most. Walk-in customers experience no negative change. No measured switching rates are established; mobile_repeat_change_pp remains null pending 30-day cohort completion.",
        "board_delta": {
          "mobile_repeat_change_pp": null,
          "public_strategy_history": [
            "Move 1 — Starbucks: Launched 50-café peak-time capacity-reservation pilot (20% reserved for café orders, 07:00–10:00 weekdays) with matched 50-comparison-café instrumentation. No baseline measurements yet complete.",
            "Move 1 — Buyer: Habitual-app and commuter segments adopt contingent switching rules; walk-in segment unaffected. Switching triggers are active but outcomes depend on slot pressure not yet measurable."
          ]
        },
        "confidence": 0.55
      },
      "branches": {
        "uncertainty": "Whether January mobile-slot demand is high enough relative to the capped mobile allocation to push offered pickup times past buyer switching thresholds for a material share of habitual-app and commuter customers. No demand baseline or slot-fill rate exists in the record.",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "low_pressure",
            "description": "Mobile allocation is sufficient for most demand; fewer than threshold-crossing events per user occur; switching is marginal. Competitors see at most a modest uptick in morning traffic attributable to diversion.",
            "probability": 0.55,
            "board_delta": {
              "mobile_repeat_change_pp": null,
              "simulated_buyer_switching_note": "Low slot pressure: minority of mobile users hit thresholds; walk-in experience improves; competitor diversion small and not yet measurable."
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "high_pressure",
            "description": "Mobile allocation fills quickly during peak; many habitual-app and commuter users receive slots beyond their stated tolerances; two-event switching thresholds crossed by a larger share; meaningful transaction diversion to Dutch Bros and Dunkin in January.",
            "probability": 0.45,
            "board_delta": {
              "mobile_repeat_change_pp": null,
              "simulated_buyer_switching_note": "High slot pressure: material share of mobile users redirects spend to competitors by end of January; measurable cohort impact expected but not yet confirmed."
            }
          }
        ],
        "played": "low_pressure"
      }
    },
    {
      "ruling_id": "r3_competitor_response",
      "basis": "judgment",
      "rationale": "Dutch Bros (m1.dutch.a1, m1.dutch.a2) and Dunkin (m1.dunkin.a1, m1.dunkin.a2, m1.dunkin.a3) both commit real resources in January 2027: Dutch Bros adds 20 labor hours/week across 12 shops (starting Jan 1) and a $1 Order Ahead credit offer (Jan 15–31, capped at 100/shop); Dunkin launches a $6 breakfast Meal Deal in 15 franchise locations with a $20,000 combined promotion-and-staffing cap. Both are within their stated envelopes (d.dutch.d5, d.dutch.d6, d.dunkin.k5, d.dunkin.k6). The key interaction is whether these offers are ready to absorb the buyer diversion that the Starbucks pilot may generate. I adjudicate their operational commitments as implemented; economic outcomes (incremental contribution, repeat lift) are unresolved because no pre-period baselines or redemption counts exist.",
      "resolves": ["m1.dutch.a1", "m1.dutch.a2", "m1.dunkin.a1", "m1.dunkin.a2", "m1.dunkin.a3"],
      "affected_actors": ["dutch", "dunkin", "buyer"],
      "cite": ["d.dutch.d4", "d.dutch.d5", "d.dutch.d6", "d.dutch.d7", "d.dutch.d8", "d.dunkin.k3", "d.dunkin.k4", "d.dunkin.k5", "d.dunkin.k6", "d.dunkin.k7", "d.dunkin.k8"],
      "chain": [
        {
          "link": "dutch_labor_commitment",
          "basis": "dossier_fact",
          "claim": "Dutch Bros commits 20 additional labor hours/week across 12 shops from Jan 1 (4 h/weekday, 07:00–10:00), costing approximately $2,400/shop/month at the $30/hour assumed rate — $28,800 total, within the $30,000 envelope (d.dutch.d5, d.dutch.d6). This is an implemented cost, not a simulated one.",
          "cite": ["d.dutch.d5", "d.dutch.d6"]
        },
        {
          "link": "dutch_credit_offer",
          "basis": "dossier_fact",
          "claim": "From Jan 15, Dutch Bros offers a $1 Order Ahead credit for first-time users, capped at 100 redemptions per shop (max $1,200 total across 12 shops). This is within the remaining budget envelope (d.dutch.d6). Redemption volume depends on whether displaced Starbucks mobile users reach these shops and adopt Order Ahead — an unresolved behavioral outcome (d.dutch.d8).",
          "cite": ["d.dutch.d6", "d.dutch.d8"]
        },
        {
          "link": "dutch_service_capacity",
          "basis": "dossier_fact",
          "claim": "Dutch Bros' drive-through model and added labor increase peak throughput capacity at these 12 shops, but whether local lane capacity is binding during the additional demand is unknown (d.dutch.d7). No spare-capacity data is supplied.",
          "cite": ["d.dutch.d7"]
        },
